module v2.workflow.floor_route_gap

import v2.std.algebra { Cons, Empty, list_flat_map }
import v2.std.collection { List }
import v2.std.text { String }

// THE EXPECTATION IS TYPED AT THE TWO FACTS THE INTERPRETER OBSERVES. An identity-only row
// says merely that some route gap once existed; it cannot object when the witness reaches a
// different operation or when the ground changes to one with a different remedy. New rows are
// authored here and projected into `floor_route_gap_roster`, so identity enrollment remains one
// derived fact rather than a second list beside the detail.
type FloorRouteGapGround =
    UnpublishedMockCase
  | NoMockResponse
  | FilesystemRemoval

type FloorRouteGapExpectation = {
  identity: String,
  operation: String,
  ground: FloorRouteGapGround
}

// THE ROUTE-GAP ROSTER: identities the required floor EXECUTES, that reach a host effect the
// hermetic route has no arm for, enrolled by exact qualified name.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. A file declaring `data live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition =
// ReadsLiveTree` had every one of its identities declined before execution
// (`RequiredFloorDisposition.DeclinedLiveTree`) -- 778 of them across 112 files when that was
// measured -- and since `claim_executor --required-floor` is the only witness-executing
// consumer in the tree, a declined identity ran nowhere at all. This roster exists to observe
// the complementary class: identities that ARE planned and executed, and whose execution then
// reaches a host effect the hermetic route has no arm for, surfaced per identity, every run,
// by the interpreter's own typed refusal.
//
// WHAT THIS ROSTER DOES NOT ESTABLISH, stated because an earlier revision of this header
// asserted exactly the opposite: it does NOT establish that every discovered identity is
// planned, and it does NOT establish whether `RequiredFloorDisposition.DeclinedLiveTree`
// exists. That revision claimed the arm was deleted and that "every discovered identity is now
// routed and executed". Both halves were false when written, falsified by any floor run whose
// receipt reports a nonzero pre-execution decline. It was rung inflation on the very axis the
// sibling authority corrects ITSELF for (`required_floor`, review 54035) -- one carrier
// narrating another's behaviour instead of reading it.
//
// THE JURISDICTION, written as a boundary rather than as a count so that no future revision has
// a place to restate the same class of claim: `required_floor` owns PRE-EXECUTION PLANNING --
// which discovered identities are declined, and why. `floor_route_gap` classifies ONE EXECUTED
// OUTCOME. A roster of executed refusals is structurally incapable of proving planning
// completeness, because every identity it can see has already been planned. So the planner's
// population is read from `v2.workflow.required_floor` and its per-identity receipt, never
// copied here -- a copy is what drifted last time, and it would drift again in the same
// direction. This paragraph stays true both before and after `DeclinedLiveTree` is deleted,
// which is the property the sentence it replaces did not have.
//
// SO THIS ROSTER IS NOT A SKIP LIST, for exactly the reason `floor_expected_red` is not one:
// every identity here EXECUTES and its outcome is still asserted. What enrollment changes is
// which outcome counts as agreement.
//
// - Enrolled, and the route gapped: agreement. Counted and held, never green.
// - NOT enrolled, and the route gapped: reds the build. A new route gap is a new fact.
// - Enrolled, and the route did NOT gap: reds the build as a stale row. A route that was
//   supplied (or a witness that stopped reaching for the effect) is counted the moment it
//   lands, so the roster shrinks monotonically instead of becoming a place rows go to be
//   forgotten.
// - Enrolled, and the identity did not execute at all: reds the build. The reverse join is
//   what keeps a renamed or deleted identity from sitting here forever, unobserved.
//
// THE ORACLE THIS ROSTER IS ALLOWED TO BE (DESIGN §5, the 2026-08-01 ruling on numeric
// literals). It is a monotone debt contract, and it is legitimate only because all four of that
// ruling's conditions hold: the subject universe is INDEPENDENTLY DISCOVERED (the floor's own
// discovered roster, not a hand-authored list of what someone remembered), it is CLOSED (every
// discovered identity takes exactly one disposition), membership is checked AT IDENTITY GRAIN
// rather than by count, and the direction is justified — every row names an operation that
// needs a route, so supplying routes empties it. There is no count assertion anywhere in this
// mechanism, and there must never be one: `planned == executed` is an identity join, and a
// population equality is not a substitute for it.
//
// A ROW IS NOT A LICENCE TO STOP. Enrolling an identity here records that the floor has no
// route for it TODAY; it does not make the gap acceptable. The remedy is always a route —
// publish the mock case, author the operation's `mock_response`, or supply a lane that can run
// the effect — never an edit to what the witness asserts.
//
// EMPTY IS THE INTENDED TERMINAL STATE, and there is deliberately no refusal on an empty
// roster. When every discovered identity has a route that runs it, this module deletes.
//
// THE POPULATION, MEASURED RATHER THAN ESTIMATED (floor run 32345970386, sha c812b9fb6d0, the
// first run in which every discovered identity was routed and executed). 157 identities reach a
// host effect the hermetic route has no arm for, and the operations they name are exactly the
// ones this corpus's own exclusion notes have been naming in prose for months:
//    54  Dir
//    26  IsExecutable
//    17  Run
//    10  Check
//    10  emit_host_native_cache_evict
//     8  Write
//     5  git.Inspect.HeadCommit
//     4  Get
//     4  Read
//     4  ReadBytes
//     3  Filesystem.List
//     3  Parents
//     2  DiffNameStatus
//     2  IsNonEmpty
//     1  KernelName
//     1  shell.Find.Files
//     1  VerifyBuildArtifacts
//     1  DiffUnified
//     1  RecursiveForce
//
// NOT ONE OF THEM IS A COMMITTED-SOURCE READ. That is the measurement that settles what the
// checkout-read carve-out actually covers: of ~783 identities admitted by deleting the
// file-grain live-tree decline, 626 EXECUTE and only these 157 genuinely need a route. The
// premise that "reads the live tree" implies "cannot run hermetically" was wrong for the large
// majority of the population it excluded, not for an interesting minority of it.
//
// 101 OF THESE 157 ARE A RECLASSIFICATION, NOT NEW DEBT, and they are the reason this module
// earns its keep rather than adding a vocabulary. They were already executing on main every
// run, already reaching a hermetic refusal — and because that refusal arrived as a
// `TypeError` carrying prose, `ExpectedRedArm` read it as an ordinary failure and HELD it as
// agreement. `v2.workflow.floor_expected_red` therefore recorded "this witness runs and fails
// and someone is fixing it" about 101 rows that never reached their subject at all. Nothing
// was fixing them because there was nothing to fix: they needed a ROUTE, not a REPAIR. The
// arithmetic is exact and is what turns this from a suspicion into a finding — that run's
// roster carried 308 enrolled identities and held 207, and the 101 difference is precisely
// this set. They are DELETED from that roster in the same change that adds them here; an
// identity enrolled in both would be two mechanisms claiming one fact.
//
// The remaining 56 are genuinely new, surfaced by admitting the previously-declined population.

